  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Complete the following reasoning about the set $S=(-\\infty ,1):$\n\nThe value $-99$ is not the minimum of the set $S$ because {{field-1}} and yet {{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that ${x}_{min}$ is the minimum of a set $S$ if \n\n- ${x}_{min}\\in S,$ and\n\n- ${x}_{min}\\le x$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nIn our case, the value $-99$ is not the minimum of the set $S$ because, for example, $-100\\in S$ and yet $-100<-99.$",
